{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"agent_message","text":"# Review Findings: Phase C4 Slice F2 — REPL Catalog Selection\n\n**Status: REJECTED**\n\n## P1 — `yin.repl` still loads the legacy frontend implementations\n\n`yin.repl` requires `yin.repl.frontends` and builds the standard catalog when `:frontends` is omitted. That namespace statically requires `yang.clojure`, `yang.python`, and `yang.php`. Ruling 16 assigns catalog construction to each host’s composition and says `yin.repl` should require no frontend. Current host entry points, including `yin.repl.main/boot`, `yin.repl.embed`, and `yin.repl.driver/create-state`, rely on the implicit default. Move default catalog construction to the composition layer and pass the catalog explicitly.\n\n## P1 — Selection does not pin a revision\n\n`select-frontend` selects a qualified id without a revision. If the catalog contains multiple revisions of that id, selection is ambiguous, and `frontend-binding` reports `unavailable-parser`. The API offers no way to select a particular revision or record a `[id revision]` selection in session state, contrary to Ruling 16. Add revision-aware selection and pin the selected revision for the request/session.\n\n## P2 — Tests leave required selection cases uncovered\n\nThe tests don’t check selection when a catalog contains multiple revisions of the same id. They also don’t demonstrate the Node and Dart lanes described in the acceptance criteria, or the explicitly configured parser-service case mentioned in the spec. Add coverage for multi-revision selection and the required host lanes; clarify whether service-aware availability is outside this slice.\n\n## P2 — Help still advertises a fixed language list\n\n`help-text` and `lang-labels` remain hardcoded to Clojure, Python, and PHP even though selection is catalog-driven. Custom frontends can be selected but aren’t reflected in help, and qualified ids are displayed without using their manifest language. This leaves stale language-specific behavior in the REPL.\n\n## Portability\n\nThe changed dispatch uses portable Clojure constructs and introduces no reflection or host-specific API. I found no direct JVM, Node, or Dart portability defect in the changed logic.\n\n## Review scope and report delivery\n\nReviewed §8.5.6/Ruling 16, the changed REPL and catalog files, the four new tests, `collab/findings.md` (the implementation report present in the worktree), and host construction call sites. I did not run tests.\n\nThe requested findings file could not be written: the workspace is configured read-only, and the attempted temporary report write was also denied."}}
